  "query_preview": "..\n\nA tranquil fall pattern continues for the next few days as high \npressure remains in control over SE Michigan. The origin of the \nsurface high however transitions away from Quebec and toward the \nemerging high pressure center over the Upper Midwest today. This \nwill reinforce the northeasterly winds that we have seen for the \npast couple of days to maintain moisture flux off of Lake Huron. A \nslight increase in wind speed and upstream moisture content over \nOntario suggests slightly more aggressive moisture flux than \nprevious mornings. That said, synoptic scale moisture is still quite \nminimal with the 27.00z KDTX sounding sampling PWAT of 0.38\" (10th \npercentile per SPC climatology). This has ultimately kept \nfog/stratus localized to the Thumb/Saginaw Valley. Similar fog/low \nstratus concerns will persist each morning until winds flip to the \nsouth Tuesday with the departure of the high. \n\nOtherwise, seasonable temperatures hold in the low 70s over the next \nseveral days as the pattern remains at a standstill due to the \nstalled Nor'Easter. No signs of any meaningful thickness advection \nuntil Tuesday when flow flips southwest as the east coast system \nfills in and absorbs into the mean flow. By Tuesday night, mid level \nflow becomes zonal which opens the Great Lakes to a low amplitude \nshortwave by early Wednesday morning. Majority of guidance brings \nthis Pacific wave and its accompanying upper level moisture across \nthe state well ahead of low level Gulf moisture. This keeps rain \nchances low (<30%) for most of Wednesday in favor of just increasing \ncloud cover and possibly some virga. There remains disagreement in \nmodel guidance regarding timing of the gulf moisture surge, which \nwill dictate when rain chances start to ramp up. This looks to be \nsometime Wednesday evening with rain chances then continuing through \nThursday. Return flow will eventually be cut off as a cold front is \ndragged across northern CONUS by a northern stream low, which also \nushers in an even cooler airmass to start off October as highs will \nonly be in the mid-60s.",
